WEBVTT

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:06.180
There are about 50 species of land mammals living wild in the British countryside.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:12.050
They're easy to overlook for they're secretive, usually silent except at the breeding season,

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.340
and often active mainly at night.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.720
They're also easily disturbed, so that usually we get only a tantalizing glimpse of them.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:29.970
And that makes their...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.940
identification difficult. But this video enables us to study the land mammals of Britain in their

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:41.680
natural habitat and become familiar with their appearance and the way they move. And we can

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.160
compare apparently similar animals. Some of our smallest mammals are shrews and mice. But if you

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:56.760
know how, it's easy to distinguish one from the other. What is the difference between a weasel

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.970
and a stoat? They're closely related but quite...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.590
different when you know what to look for. This is an otter, but what other British

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:14.330
mammals regularly go for a swim? Watching our wild animals is undoubtedly more

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:18.750
difficult than watching wild birds, but it's very rewarding and it can add a

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.170
great deal to our enjoyment of the British countryside.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:38.800
The best places to see our largest native animals are undoubtedly in the Scottish Highlands.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:45.840
This is where red deer still live in the wild, often in quite large herds, the sex is usually segregated.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.050
In summer, they congregate high up above the tree line, feeding on the new growth.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.930
But in winter, they descend to lower grasslands and woods, where there is both food and shelter.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.960
Those magnificent antlers are grown each year.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.880
having been discarded in the spring.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.200
There are other places in Britain where you can see red deer,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.620
for they've been kept in parks for centuries,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.620
and some have become re-established in the countryside.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.870
Their large size, up to four feet 130 centimeters at the shoulder,

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.990
helps to distinguish them from other deer.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.550
So does their red-brown coat, which has no spots.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.960
One point to watch when identifying any of our deer...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.540
is the pale patch on the rump, which, in the case of red deer, extends right up to include the tail.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:49.130
The noisiest time of the year for red deer is at the end of September and early October,

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.270
mating time, when the sexes are seen together.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.100
In deer, this period is called the rut.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.730
The stags round up as many hinds as possible and defend them against other challenges.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:27.050
Sometimes fierce battles ensue between challenging stags.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:49.070
Usually it's a trial of strength, with no serious injuries, a weaker stag retiring when

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:50.690
it accepts its inferiority.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.680
In some parts of the country, such as the New Forest,

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:08.090
you can see both red deer as well as their smaller relatives

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:12.480
introduced from the Far East, Sika deer.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.120
These are smaller than red deer and in summer have spots on their coats,

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.400
although that may be hard to see in the autumn and winter.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.250
At the rutting time, the stags have a distinctive whistle.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:42.590
As well as in Dorset and Hampshire, there are feral herds in West Yorkshire, Scotland and Ireland.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.990
They're only about two-thirds the size of red deer.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.540
Another recognition point is the pale patch on their rumps.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:55.830
Unlike red deer, this doesn't extend upwards to include the tail and has a more distinctive heart shape.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:09.150
Another deer common in the New Forest area, but which you might see almost anywhere in England, Wales and Ireland, is the fallow deer.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:17.360
The herds in the New Forest are directly descended from the animals introduced into this country in the Middle Ages from Europe, probably by the Normans.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:22.680
They're common in parklands, and many have escaped into the open countryside.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:29.810
The males of fallow deer are called bucks, smaller than red deer.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.830
but about the same size as Seeker, mature bucks have splendid antlers with a shape that

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:40.120
distinguishes them clearly from other species. From the age of three or more years, there's

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:51.580
a broad flat area on the antler which no other deer has. They too cast their antlers each

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:57.450
year, usually between April and June. Generally, the older animals cast their antlers before

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:59.970
for the younger ones and it's not uncommon to

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:12.010
see an animal with its antlers half cast. The antlers soon start to grow again, and

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:16.790
then they're covered with a protective skin with its own blood supply, feeding the developing

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:23.590
bone with calcium and oxygen. By early summer, the females, the does, will be dropping their

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:28.850
fawns deep in the forest or in high grass, which provides good cover. For a while, the

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:29.830
fawns spotted

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:38.170
coat acts as a camouflage as long as it remains still. Sometimes a doe will have two fawns with

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:45.920
her, one just born and a hanger-on from the previous year. By early summer the antlers on

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.820
the bucks have grown considerably. The skin which protects them at this stage is known as velvet

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:56.020
and it often attracts flies where it's been broken. The insects are at their greatest numbers

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.440
in the heat of midday and then the birds move in to collect them.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.040
Eventually, the velvet withers and dies,

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:14.050
and by October, the bucks are at the peak of their sexual activity for the rut.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:19.270
In wild forest herds, the master buck remains firmly in charge of his does

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.750
and can usually cope with the challenges of competitors.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:26.340
In this open park situation, however, the deer are too crowded

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.220
and the master buck can't drive away all other bucks.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.440
Nonetheless, challenges continue to be roared.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.840
In park herds, there is a lot of inbreeding,

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.160
and colour variations are common, both light and dark.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.740
But all fallow deer have a characteristic rump marking.

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:59.700
The white patch is bordered by a black, almost heart-shaped line.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:16.480
In contrast to fallow deer, roe deer are usually rather solitary in their habits,

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.580
living in woodlands and fields where there's plenty of cover.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.440
They also have a pale rump patch, white in the female, less conspicuous in the male.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:29.980
When alarmed, this shows up like a powder puff, similar to sea.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:39.430
markings but the roe appears to have almost no tail. Roe also give a distinctive bark. Another

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:49.920
way to recognize roe is by the black nose and white chin. Apart from red deer, roe are the only

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.880
truly native British deer and they're easily the smallest of the ones we've seen so far,

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.860
less than 30 inches at the shoulder. They're mating

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.160
behavior is rather unusual. In the summer, the male may follow the female round and round

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:11.780
a bush or group of small trees, producing well-trodden tracks called rings. The significance

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:23.340
of this behavior is not fully understood. Eventually, mating takes place. Roe are by no means the

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:28.090
smallest deer you might come across, as there are some introduced species, notably Chinese

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:29.610
water deer and muntjac.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.710
only 20 or so inches, 50 centimeters high.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:36.940
They occur in southeast England, having escaped from deer parks.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.500
There's one other animal that might conceivably be confused with a deer,

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:46.300
though not for long, the red-necked wallaby.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:50.400
A native of Australia, it can be seen occasionally in the Peak District

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:53.770
and in an area of Sussex south of Crawley New Town.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:59.410
These animals, of course, are escapees.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:09:59.970
They...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.640
got out of private zoos around 1940 and their numbers have fluctuated with hard winters since

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:10.520
that time. Winter is, in fact, a good time to see them when there's less cover around.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:24.080
Ringing along our roads at night, you can often see one of our most widespread and common mammals,

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:29.940
the hedgehog. Unfortunately, a squashed hedgehog is also an all-too-common

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:38.360
sight on the roads. The hedgehog, however, is safe from most animals, except foxes and

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:43.680
badgers, on account of its ability to roll up into an impregnable ball. It also does

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:48.930
this when it hibernates from October to March. In the spring, as soon as the weather becomes

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:54.410
warmer, the hedgehog emerges over a two-hour period, during which its temperature, heartbeat

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.250
and breathing return to normal.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.720
During the winter, it will have lost a third of its body weight,

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:11.420
and its first priority is to feed as fast as it can.

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:14.550
And it's then that every gardener should welcome a hedgehog,

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:17.910
for it prefers the very creatures that we regard as pests.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:21.300
A host of caterpillars, beetles, snails and slugs.

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:26.110
A hedgehog in your garden is cheaper and more efficient than slug pellets,

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:27.890
and a great deal more attractive.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:41.640
By late April, the hedgehogs will have reached normal body weight and be ready to breed.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:46.440
Although it's a familiar animal, its courtship and mating have seldom been observed.

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:52.950
As with most hedgehog activity, it usually occurs at night, and it starts with the male in pursuit.

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:05.600
The courtship is a lengthy and rather noisy affair.

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:10.970
The male may go on circling the female on and off for several hours, trying to arouse her.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:27.050
There's an old joke which goes, how to hedgehog's mate, to which the answer is, of course, with extreme caution.

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:28.700
But there's more to it than that.

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:29.960
It's impossible for...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.520
a male hedgehog to mate unless the female is receptive and willing to place herself correctly

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:40.960
for him she does this by lying with her hind legs spread out her back arched inwards and the

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:56.590
hitherto protective spines laid flat the male seizes her by the scruff of the neck and mating

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.960
takes place. A hedgehog's belly skin is very thin

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.200
And if the female did not flatten her prickles, the male could be badly hurt.

00:13:05.290 --> 00:13:07.940
Mating lasts only a minute, and the pair separate.

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:11.390
The male plays no further part in bringing up the family.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:13.670
Indeed, the two animals may never meet again.

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:23.740
The female needs to mate several times before conceiving, so it's usually May before she becomes pregnant.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.900
She then seeks out a quiet secluded corner in which to give birth, like this man-made shelter.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.100
under a pile of gardening odds and ends.

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:40.110
She lines the nest with dry materials, dead grass and leaves.

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:43.330
The babies are due about five weeks after mating.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:58.880
As the time approaches, the female becomes very restless.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.960
Giving birth involves much heaving and straining.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:07.640
But mercifully, the mother doesn't have to give birth to an infant covered with sharp prickles.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:13.400
The back of this very new baby is dotted with little bumps, rather like goose pimples,

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:15.800
from which spines will soon emerge.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:23.660
A second youngster is on its way.

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:28.610
But already, only a couple of hours after birth, the first baby looks very different.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:29.940
Its sparse...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:39.320
blunt spines are sticking up from its back. For three or four weeks the young are fed wholly

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:43.740
on their mother's milk, but then the mother starts leading her family away from the nest

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:49.820
on foraging excursions. By the time the leaves are starting to change colour, hedgehogs are

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:55.740
beginning to build up their fat reserves to enable them to hibernate through the winter. Individuals

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:59.350
from late litters may not have time to accumulate enough.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.920
and may not, therefore, survive the winter.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:06.480
If you enjoy having hedgehogs in your garden, this is where you can help.

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:09.960
It's not difficult to find them at night with a torch or lantern,

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:13.020
and if you discover one that looks small during the autumn,

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.000
pick it up and weigh it.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:19.310
450 grams is the crucial weight. That's about a pound.

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:22.180
If your hedgehog weighs less than that,

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:24.820
it's almost certain to die during the coming winter.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:27.640
So by feeding it, you will at least give it a chance.

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:29.970
When it's ready to hibernate, it will...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:37.700
do so anyway. The old country tradition about hedgehogs sucking milk from cows has never

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:42.720
been substantiated, but there's no doubt that hedgehogs like milk. If you have hedgehogs

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:47.030
in your garden, try putting out a bowl of bread and milk regularly for them. You may

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:51.360
then have the pleasure of watching a hedgehog from the comfort of your house, and do the

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:53.420
animals a good turn at the same time.

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:11.750
Whilst the presence of hedgehogs in the garden may quite rightly be actively encouraged,

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:16.700
it has to be admitted that the perpetrator of this requires some forbearance.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.500
There's not much mistaking the signs of moles.

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:24.880
They can be found where the soil is suitable for burrowing almost anywhere in Britain except Ireland.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:29.980
They are therefore common in pasture, gardens, parks and in woodland.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.140
though the signs are less easy to spot there.

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:35.320
The mole is extremely difficult to observe

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:37.740
since it leaves an almost totally underground existence.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.460
If you're fortunate enough to spot one active near the surface,

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.990
stand very still, otherwise the vibrations of your feet

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.830
will send it scuttling away underground.

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:56.920
Moles are active both day and night,

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:59.220
alternating between digging and resting.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.960
Each mole is the sole occupant of a whole tunnel system.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:10.060
In pasture, these may stretch for about 30 to 40 metres, in the case of females, and rather longer for males.

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:17.440
Moles live almost entirely on earthworms, but they will also take leather jackets and other small invertebrates.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:21.260
Although their eyes are functional, they're of little use underground,

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:26.540
so they locate food with a long tapering snout, which has a highly sensitive tip.

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:38.000
At less than half the size of a mole, the common shrew spends most of its time well hidden in the grass and leaves.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:41.580
It can be found almost everywhere in Britain, but not in Ireland.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:49.510
It's that long, flexible, pointed snout which clearly distinguishes the shrews from other small mammals.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:59.290
Clearly, it's partial to juicy worms, but shrews feed on a wide variety of small snails and insects, as well as wood lice and grass seeds.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.360
And like moles, they feed almost incessantly, day and night.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.740
They have a musky odour, which makes them repellent to some predators.

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:15.500
Nevertheless, your cat will kill them, although it won't eat them.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:20.490
And if your cat brings in a shrew that's less than two inches, six centimetres overall,

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:24.390
it's probably a close relative of the common shrew, the pygmy shrew.

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:29.960
If you see a shrew swimming, it's almost certain to be...

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.980
a water shrew. That's the largest of our shrews, measuring up to three and three-quarter inches,

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:40.460
about nine and a half centimeters. It feeds on various aquatic insects and the remains of other

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:46.940
large animals. And when numbers are high, as with all shrews, fights start easily. The water

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:51.980
shrew's dark upper parts and light underside help to distinguish them from other shrews when

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:54.740
they're found away from water, which happens quite frequently.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:07.260
Although common and widespread, shrews are too small and secretive to be easily observed.

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:09.630
But rabbits are a different matter.

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:12.840
You can often see them from the roadside or even from a train,

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:15.790
but getting close for observation is more difficult,

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:20.180
for they have a formidable array of senses with which to detect potential danger.

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:29.430
Their ideal habitat is small pasture fields bounded by hedge banks.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.440
but they're widespread almost everywhere, except high mountains and coniferous forests.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.720
They were probably introduced to Britain in Norman times,

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:44.310
and by the 1940s had reached a staggering population of 60 to 100 million.

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:48.240
In the 1950s, they were afflicted with myxomatosis,

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.180
and their numbers were sharply reduced,

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:53.000
but they're still one of our most familiar larger animals.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:57.160
They've colonized many of our islands, too,

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:59.970
and on some, such as Scoma off the Welsh coast,

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.930
they share the cliffs with seabirds. The puffins often make use of the rabbits

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:12.780
burrows for their nests. In closed rabbit populations such as these, color

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:17.700
mutations often arise. These natural varieties have enabled breeders to

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:27.220
produce numerous domestic strains, but these are truly wild rabbits. Rabbits

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:29.780
live in colonies and a typical breeding group consists

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.630
of about three adult bucks and up to five does they live in a world dominated by smell and

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:40.800
they're constantly interacting and checking each other's identity and sexual condition

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:46.960
territories around the home warren have definite boundaries which are patrolled and sent marked by

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:55.380
members of the community rabbits produce two kinds of dropping one kind small moist pellets

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:59.980
still rich in protein is taken directly into the mouth to be chewed again and redigested

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:10.310
The other, more familiar kind, is dry and compact and dropped at traditional sites known as latrines.

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:14.550
The surrounding area is daubed with scent from glands beneath the chin.

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:24.000
The latrines act as information centres providing the colony with up to the minute chemical news

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:27.500
about the sex, age and social status of the colony members.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:29.980
If you see a...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:35.700
rabbit doing this, it may be digging up edible roots. But the same action is very often employed

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:41.460
as a threat display between rivals. Dominant bucks will patrol the borders and usually see

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:47.330
off any bachelor males wishing to approach the resident does. But sometimes, challenges collide

00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:59.970
in fierce battles. Fur can fly as...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.090
rabbits bite and kick long needle sharp claws can rip open flesh and chisel like teeth deliver

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:11.920
slashing bites they will sometimes fight to the death and dominant bucks show little mercy to

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:25.800
their enemies banishing them to a life of exile on the edge of the community in the territory

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:29.950
of a breeding group, dominant males have priority of access to females.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.250
entering a period of fertility. During courtship, the buck pursues the doe,

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:51.500
nuzzling her back and attempting to mate with her. The doe excavates a nest

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:56.470
chamber, or stop, where she will give birth to her kittens. Stops within the

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:59.850
warren offer best protection to the young, so does frequently

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:08.670
squabble over these prime nesting sites. The doe gathers bundles of nesting

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:13.920
material with surprising dexterity and ingenuity. Fur plucked from her body will

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:18.780
complete the lining to the nest. A doe may have up to five litters, all in

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:27.000
different nests, in the main breeding season from February to September. One

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:29.960
month after a successful mating, a pregnant doe

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.760
will give birth in the stop at night.

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:34.180
The kittens will soon be left alone,

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:36.600
huddled together for warmth and comfort.

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:39.870
To minimise the amount of attention drawn to the stop,

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:43.470
the doe visits her kittens only once every 24 hours

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:44.710
for a few minutes suckling.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:47.980
Afterwards, she meticulously covers up

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:49.400
and camouflages the entrance,

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:51.580
scratching and trampling the earth

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:54.820
to make it appear just another area of poor-scraped ground.

00:23:54.820 --> 00:23:57.950
This helps eliminate any lingering odours,

00:23:58.340 --> 00:23:59.820
a necessary precaution against...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:08.890
discovery by prowling predators. Later, the doe will move her young to the main

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:12.530
warren, from whence they will set off on their first venture into the outside

00:24:12.530 --> 00:24:16.870
world. It's a measure of the rabbit's phenomenal fertility that these

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:20.890
youngsters, within five months, may be caring for a litter of their own.

00:24:24.590 --> 00:24:29.390
Although this is a typical rabbit habitat, brown hares may also be seen on such

00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:29.970
grazing land.

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:34.960
They're widespread in Britain too, although they tend to favour agricultural land,

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:39.500
particularly the plains of the eastern counties and the limestone uplands of the south.

00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:46.470
With a little practice, you can easily distinguish them from rabbits by their longer bodied, longer legs and longer ears.

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:51.440
Their ears also have black tips, unlike rabbits.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:59.980
A hare has a loping gait and its tail, although white underneath, hasn't got the white powder puff appearance of a rabbit.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.770
It's this sort of thing that has earned them the nickname of Mad March hares,

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:11.000
although you could see these antics at any time during the long breeding season between January and August.

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:19.600
Several males close to a female may become involved in a mating chase,

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:24.550
but there's usually a dominant guarding male who attempts to chase other males away.

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:29.980
It's the boxing which is most likely to catch our eye at a distance.

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:38.940
Sometimes it's the males sparring for a female, but we know that females also frequently box with males to reject unwanted suitors.

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:54.940
In the Scottish Highlands, as well as the Peak District in North Wales, you may be lucky enough to see mountain hares.

00:25:55.420 --> 00:25:59.980
They're less timid than brown hares. This one is in its winter coat, but instead of the winter coat,

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:04.220
In the summer, it becomes similar to the brown hare, although it's stockier and its ears are shorter.

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:08.440
There are mountain hares in Ireland, too, but they don't turn white in winter.

00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:12.800
Scotland is the best place to see our native red squirrel.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:18.520
You'll most likely find it in conifer woods or mixed woods where there's a large proportion of conifers.

00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:24.900
You'll recognise it by its chestnut red coat and tail, the underparts of which can be quite creamy in the summer.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:29.980
Outside Scotland, the red squirrel can now only be found in certain areas.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:36.130
localities, parts of North Wales, East Anglia and Northern England. Other isolated communities

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:42.140
occur in such places as the Isle of Wight and Brownsea Island in Pool Harbour. The red squirrel

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:48.060
has long distinctive ear tufts, which identify it beyond doubt. Without those, you could

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:53.090
confuse it with the grey squirrel in winter, for then a grey tinge can develop in its fur.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.980
Squirrels tend to be more active in the morning and evening, but you can see them any time of the day.

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:10.000
Their food is varied, but in pinewoods, fur cones provide their staple food.

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:29.980
Fur cones are also attacked by birds, such as crossbills, but they just split the scales.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:38.870
leaving a ragged cone, whilst the squirrel eats its way steadily through it, leaving only a clean-cut core.

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:41.940
This one has definitely been eaten by a squirrel.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:47.810
Grey squirrels are larger than reds, and a good deal more widespread.

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:55.700
They were introduced here from North America, and today live in much of England and Wales, Central Ireland, and the central lowlands of Scotland.

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.810
Unlike the red squirrels, they frequent parklands.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.750
and they especially like chestnut, oak, beech, and hazel trees.

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:14.490
Generally, their upper parts are grey, but there is some yellowish-brown along the back,

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:20.230
and some chestnut colouration on flanks and limbs in summer, which could cause some confusion

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:22.370
if you only get a fleeting glimpse of one.

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:29.980
They build large leafy nests in the trees, sometimes in the branches, and some...

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:35.240
sometimes in tree hollows. There are two litters during the first six months of the year and the

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:41.120
young are born blind, deaf and naked, though by the time they're 14 days old they will have a downy

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:49.380
coat. The breeding dray is lined with a thick layer of soft material such as grass, leaves and

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:54.890
moss, but much of the nest is built from young shoots and green leaves, the collection of which

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:59.980
inevitably injures tree growth. Gray squirrels are therefore not loved by

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.620
foresters. Their worst offence is the habit of bark stripping. They discard the

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:11.440
bark but feed on the trees sap underneath. It can happen during midsummer and also

00:29:11.440 --> 00:29:17.990
during a severe winter. It may be done to combat drought or to obtain extra

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:29.920
vitamins. Gray squirrels come down to the

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.820
to berry nuts and acorns, which they will find again by scent during the winter when food is short.

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:40.710
And in open woodlands you may see them travelling quite long distances over ground

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:43.080
with that distinctive jumping run.

00:29:48.290 --> 00:29:51.090
We have many different kinds of small mammals in Britain,

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:54.390
but each tends to favour a slightly different kind of habitat.

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:59.960
One species in particular likes coppices formed by the ancient rotational method of crop.

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:06.490
During the cold months of the year, it hibernates in a nest at or below ground level.

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:10.620
It's the dormouse, not a true mouse, but a close relative.

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:15.100
During hibernation, the dormouse's respiration and heart rate fall,

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:18.950
its temperature drops, even the composition of its blood is altered.

00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:22.760
The return to normal takes up to twelve hours

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:25.220
and imposes a great strain on the animal.

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:29.970
It will have lost hundreds of years.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.740
its body weight and now it must feed rapidly on green shoots, grass seeds and

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:39.680
buds before it can begin breeding. Now we can see its recognizable features,

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:45.950
orange-brown fur and bushy tail which no mouse possesses. The tail helps it to

00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:55.210
balance when climbing. The breeding nest is usually about a meter off the ground,

00:30:55.600 --> 00:30:59.620
a rather untidy structure often made of honeysuckle bark or dry grass.

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:25.910
Inside her nest the Dormouse gets ready to give birth. The mother herself is small a little over three inches seven and a half centimeters

00:31:25.910 --> 00:31:29.960
But this scrap of life blind and naked

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:46.200
weighs only three grams. It almost looks like a premature birth. As each baby is

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:51.950
born, the mother picks it up and cleans it thoroughly. Four is the average number in a litter.

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:03.990
The eyes of the babies don't open until they're 18 days old.

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:09.110
They'll remain in the nest for a month, much longer than any other small species.

00:32:13.330 --> 00:32:16.700
For weeks, the young may begin to make excursions from the nest.

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:19.180
They have greyer coats than their mother.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:22.720
As a rule, dormice are only active at night.

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:26.500
Their large eyes and long whiskers help them to find their way through the branches.

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:29.980
But young dormice not uncommonly emerge before it.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:34.830
dark they're well suited to a climbing way of life apart from the tail for

00:32:34.830 --> 00:32:39.530
balance both front and back feet are adapted for grasping and they have small

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:53.420
pads on them to keep a firm grip as autumn approaches dormice eat in real

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:58.180
earnest particularly nuts which contain plenty of protein and fat and enable

00:32:58.180 --> 00:32:59.800
them to put on reserves for the winter

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:07.460
They turn the nut round, cutting through the shell with their lower teeth before removing the kernel bit by bit.

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:13.580
A neatly opened nut with a smoothly chiseled edge is a real doormouse trademark.

00:33:15.400 --> 00:33:21.860
The edible doormouse is very different from the common doormouse, being about twice the size and having no orange in its coat.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:25.620
It could be confused with a grey squirrel, but it's much smaller.

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:29.400
It's an introduced species found only in the area of the Chiltons.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:34.000
And there, it frequently gets into house lofts, where it can cause some annoyance.

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:36.910
Sometimes it hibernates in roofs, too.

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:41.520
They're called edible dormice because they were once a favourite food of the Romans,

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:46.280
who reared them in oak and beech groves, fattening them up on currants and chestnuts.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:58.960
Unlike the edible dormice, many of our small mammals are very widely distributed,

00:33:59.280 --> 00:33:59.980
occurring up...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:06.820
and down the country in our fields and hedges. Among the most abundant are the voles. Bank voles

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:12.810
usually prefer thick cover and generally don't move far into the fields. All voles have blunt

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:18.340
slouts compared to other small mammals, and the bank vole has fairly prominent ears and a reddish

00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:29.560
brown back. Field voles are very similar, although their coloration is greyish brown. They're found

00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:29.820
everywhere.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:34.970
except in Ireland and on some islands they feed mainly on green leaves and

00:34:34.970 --> 00:34:39.390
stems of grasses and growing corn and they're active throughout the 24 hours

00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:52.720
but mostly at night you have to be an expert to distinguish field voles from

00:34:52.720 --> 00:34:57.200
bank voles but apart from the slight difference in coloration field voles have

00:34:57.200 --> 00:34:59.160
less conspicuous ears and a shorter tail

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:08.870
Our two small species of vole are preyed on by many kinds of birds, as well as larger mammals.

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:14.790
But we also have a much larger vole, and this is its typical habitat.

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:20.510
Lowland rivers, such as chalk streams, as well as ponds and canals in England, Wales and Scotland.

00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:25.580
This is the home of the water vole, the Mr Ratty of the wind in the willows.

00:35:26.240 --> 00:35:29.760
But although this animal is sometimes mistakenly called a water rat,

00:35:30.160 --> 00:35:32.040
Rat, it most certainly is not.

00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:36.090
It's about 9 inches, 23 centimeters, from nose to tail,

00:35:36.270 --> 00:35:40.740
and its typical blunt snout indicates clearly that it's a vole.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:48.660
Voles can remain submerged for up to 30 seconds,

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:53.010
and they're lithe and graceful swimmers, although their feet are not webbed.

00:35:56.580 --> 00:35:59.780
Every vole constructs its own elaborate system of burrows.

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:04.460
You have to be still and patient to observe water voles because they're very timid.

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:06.940
But you can easily see evidence of their presence.

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:09.110
The banks are riddled with holes.

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:13.300
Each system contains a nest chamber and emergency exits.

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:16.420
And at least one of the three entrances will be underwater.

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:29.960
And here is another sign of water voles, a boundary marker dividing the range of one territorial male.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:36.240
from another. The droppings on the log advertise that this male is in residence and male callers

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:46.630
are unwelcome. The water vole feeds close to home and uses its paws rather like a squirrel to eat

00:36:46.630 --> 00:36:51.570
any vegetation it can safely reach. Indeed everything within easy nibbling distance of

00:36:51.570 --> 00:36:56.390
the feeding hole is soon consumed and the resulting patch of closely cropped grass is

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:58.250
known as a water vole lawn.

00:37:03.450 --> 00:37:08.630
Other signs of water vole activity are the runs which tunnel through the bankside undergrowth.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:14.070
Voles are extremely short-sighted and the well-trodden waterside paths help to keep

00:37:14.070 --> 00:37:19.220
them on the right tracks. The runs are marked with a personal scent band which announces

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:29.540
individual territory within a community to any intruder. Even underwater they tend to follow the

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:29.960
same for

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.360
channels between the weeds, and as they enter an underwater burrow, they

00:37:34.360 --> 00:37:38.660
deliberately stir up a muddy smoke screen to confuse any lurking predators.

00:37:43.210 --> 00:37:47.900
Occasionally the brown rat is found in the same habitat as the water vole, but a

00:37:47.900 --> 00:37:52.450
rat, given the choice, although it can swim, prefers not to go into the water.

00:37:58.510 --> 00:37:59.830
The water vole, on the other hand,

00:38:00.090 --> 00:38:01.930
is totally at home in the water.

00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:05.000
Its short snout, giving it an appealing look,

00:38:05.550 --> 00:38:07.870
immediately distinguishes it from its greatest enemy,

00:38:08.290 --> 00:38:10.090
which so often takes over its burrows.

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:16.320
Brown rats, common rat.

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:18.980
They're found in most parts of the British Isles

00:38:18.980 --> 00:38:20.760
and are more abundant out in the countryside

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:21.960
than many people imagine.

00:38:22.760 --> 00:38:25.020
Burrows are generally in sloping ground,

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:26.840
banks or sides of ditches.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.480
Compared to the water bowl, the brown rat has a pointed muzzle and prominent ears.

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:41.460
These rats are thought to have first appeared in Britain at the beginning of the 18th century,

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:43.380
possibly in shipping from Russia.

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:46.580
Ever since, they have lived close to man.

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:51.620
Anywhere, there's food stores, scraps, rubbish tips, and, of course, sewers.

00:38:57.910 --> 00:38:59.980
Brown rats have now largely replaced...

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:04.380
the black rat which once used to be widespread in britain and which carried the black death

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:10.680
in fact its color varies but compared with the brown the black rat's ears are longer and almost

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:18.660
hairless and its eyes are bigger too mice are close relatives of rats and are among the commonest

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:24.460
british mammals in country places and the most widespread species is found in the fields hedgerows

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:28.950
woods or gardens. It's the woodmouse or long-tailed fieldmouse.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:34.740
in fact is about as long as the animal is from snout to base of tail around three and a quarter

00:39:34.740 --> 00:39:40.360
inches eight and a quarter centimeters. Several mice may live together in underground burrows or

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:47.050
under tree roots and stores of food appear to be communal nuts buds fruit bulbs almost anything

00:39:47.050 --> 00:39:54.930
apart from its long tail you'll recognize it by its large prominent eyes and long oval ears

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:59.910
ideal for its nocturnal habits. Gardeners are

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.300
often thrown into despair by their activities.

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:05.900
At night, they'll make short work of your newly sown peas

00:40:05.900 --> 00:40:08.040
unless you first soak them in paraffin.

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:19.480
Our smallest mammal is also a mouse.

00:40:19.820 --> 00:40:23.200
It's absent from Ireland and most abundant in the south of England,

00:40:23.540 --> 00:40:27.980
living in dense grasses, reed beds, and in summer in cereal crops.

00:40:29.080 --> 00:40:29.980
It's the highest...

00:40:30.920 --> 00:40:37.800
Just six grams in weight and five centimetres or so, a couple of inches, from snout to base of tail.

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:45.900
It's an active little creature and light enough to be perfectly at home on a single corn head, where it feeds in the summer.

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:54.490
It's distinguished from other mice by its blunt muzzle and small hairy ears, not unlike the voles.

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:04.550
This use of its tail is also typical.

00:41:08.690 --> 00:41:12.270
It's thought that both the number and distribution of harvest mice

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:14.990
have substantially decreased during this century,

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:18.270
and this may be due to mechanisation of farming methods,

00:41:18.450 --> 00:41:23.290
including seed drills which produce widely and evenly spaced corn stalks.

00:41:24.530 --> 00:41:29.050
It's the only British mouse to build its nest well above ground level in the stalks.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:40.000
of grass and provides a home for a litter of five to nine young another species of mouse is also

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:45.780
found in open fields but prefers the immediate neighborhood of buildings the house mouse these

00:41:48.490 --> 00:41:53.210
were probably the first mammals to be introduced into britain by human beings and they've stayed

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:59.960
close to man ever since. They have a distinctive grayish-brown color and the

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:02.840
The eyes and ears are smaller than those of a woodmouse.

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:08.490
House mice can breed the year through.

00:42:09.410 --> 00:42:13.030
In houses, they average about five litters a year, with five to a litter.

00:42:13.730 --> 00:42:18.730
The young are weaned at 18 days and are themselves able to breed at only six weeks of age.

00:42:22.600 --> 00:42:28.540
The fact that we're not overrun by mice is due, at least partly, to the large number of domestic cats,

00:42:28.790 --> 00:42:29.970
which make most of their kids...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.640
by intercepting mice, travelling between buildings

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:34.460
or moving into cover from the fields.

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:37.430
But the only truly wild cats we have

00:42:37.430 --> 00:42:40.170
are now found only in the Scottish Highlands,

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:41.680
and they are rarely seen.

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:44.460
They are larger than domestic cats,

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:47.220
but can easily be confused with those that have gone wild

00:42:47.220 --> 00:42:48.800
and reverted to a tabby form.

00:42:49.570 --> 00:42:52.810
True wild cats have a distinctive thick bushy tail

00:42:52.810 --> 00:42:54.730
with a blunt black tip.

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:12.040
We all know that our domestic cats lead independent lives and their numbers are so huge that they have to be regarded as a major predator of Britain's small mammal population, whether they live in the country or town.

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:20.570
And in their wanderings, they undoubtedly come into contact with other wild mammals. In such circumstances, they're clearly well able to look after themselves.

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:29.970
There's little evidence that foxes attack cats and out in the country, their largest...

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:35.690
prey would normally be the rabbit. They set about catching these in an apparently casual way.

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:01.660
Thank you.

00:44:02.170 --> 00:44:02.590
Thank you.

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:19.930
Foxes are found almost everywhere on the British mainland and in Ireland and in recent times have become more abundant in urban areas.

00:44:20.470 --> 00:44:25.970
A study of the foxes in Bristol has shown that there's a population of about 500 foxes there.

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:29.900
Although they are frequently

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:34.360
seen in the early hours of daylight in cities, the foxes are mainly nocturnal.

00:44:34.600 --> 00:44:39.100
scavenging from dustbins, tips, litter boxes and garden bird tables.

00:44:59.210 --> 00:44:59.930
Foxes are out there.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:01.540
noisiest in midwinter.

00:45:02.100 --> 00:45:06.280
The barks and weird, almost unearthly screeches are connected with mating.

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:10.080
For it's at this time of year that foxes embark on their courtship.

00:45:24.410 --> 00:45:27.890
At this mating time, you're quite likely to see foxes in daylight.

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:30.000
They become almost oblivious today.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.840
surroundings, the vixen leading the dog fox around for up to three days.

00:45:34.730 --> 00:45:37.590
It's an elaborate courtship, but biologically necessary.

00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:41.170
for the vixen is fertile for only a very short period.

00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:45.850
And the dog fox has no more than a few hours, once a year, to mate successfully.

00:45:49.450 --> 00:45:51.850
Seven and a half weeks later, the cubs are born.

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:55.400
in cities, in a disused cellar or under a garden shed.

