00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:32.560
one of a group of closely related animals

00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:35.000
of which the largest members are the otter and badger.

00:47:36.010 --> 00:47:39.610
The stoat feeds on rabbits and other smaller mammals and birds,

00:47:39.850 --> 00:47:44.550
and you could easily confuse it with its close relative, the weasel, which has a similar shape.

00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:49.900
The stoat, however, is larger and has a long tail with a distinctive black tip.

00:47:57.860 --> 00:47:59.320
its long, thin shape.

00:47:59.530 --> 00:47:59.870
enables

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:13.680
it to explore underground burrows and crevices and stone walls in its hunt for prey. This

00:48:13.680 --> 00:48:19.560
is a weasel in a typical posture. It's smaller than the stoat. Head and body measure about

00:48:19.560 --> 00:48:25.320
8 inches, 20 centimetres, compared with a stoat's length of about 12 inches, 30 centimetres,

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:29.970
without its tail. The weasel is altogether a more sinuous animal, although...

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:36.000
its markings are similar to those of a stoat, but it has a much shorter tail and no black tip to it.

00:48:36.120 --> 00:48:45.440
Tall cats were once widespread in mainland Britain, but are now confined to Wales and

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:50.750
adjacent counties. They can be distinguished by their creamy fur over much of the body

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:57.360
with some long dark guard hairs, but there are also escaped ferrets around that have bred with

00:48:57.360 --> 00:48:59.980
them to form hybrids, and many of the sightings are...

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:07.680
undoubtedly of such animals. Another relative, which was once widespread, is

00:49:07.680 --> 00:49:12.400
now confined to the Scottish Highlands, Wales, Ireland and the Lake District, the

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:18.460
Pine Martin. It has a distinctive pale throat and projecting ears. It's also

00:49:18.460 --> 00:49:22.760
larger and darker than a polecat, with which it could be only confused on the

00:49:22.760 --> 00:49:25.760
ground. Only rarely are they seen in good light.

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:37.320
One member of the weasel family is amphibious, equally at home on land or in water.

00:49:37.980 --> 00:49:43.500
It is, of course, the otter, one of our most attractive mammals, but now, sadly, one of our scarcest.

00:49:44.310 --> 00:49:48.030
Fortunately, there are still some remote places where you have a good chance of seeing them,

00:49:48.190 --> 00:49:50.750
and the best of these is undoubtedly in the Shetlands.

00:50:03.450 --> 00:50:08.670
By waiting very patiently by the shore, you could have more than a glimpse of an otter fishing.

00:50:09.390 --> 00:50:13.010
This is a dog otter, characterised by its broader snout.

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:24.520
This is the female, narrower in the snout.

00:50:25.120 --> 00:50:29.520
She's just about to lead her cubs from their hole down to the water for the first time.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:31.820
They are about ten weeks old.

00:50:32.480 --> 00:50:36.900
After mating has taken place, the male has little to do with bringing up the family,

00:50:41.680 --> 00:50:43.920
who seems to be an average litter for otters.

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:47.260
The young tend to stay with their mother for at least a year,

00:50:47.420 --> 00:50:49.300
and they remain a close family unit,

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:52.120
rarely going more than a few yards away from one another,

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:53.360
even when they're well grown.

00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:29.360
When a small or even half-grown otter cub gets separated from its mother, it gets worried

00:51:29.360 --> 00:51:29.960
and then becomes...

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:31.100
comes very vocal.

00:52:10.870 --> 00:52:13.170
Otter cubs can swim instinctively.

00:52:13.340 --> 00:52:16.300
But they're not very good at first, and they certainly can't dive.

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:19.380
Their coats appear to be too fluffy and buoyant.

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:21.930
And they're rather fat little things at this stage.

00:52:22.580 --> 00:52:24.920
Their mother appears to be giving them swimming lessons,

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:27.680
ducking them under the water to teach them to dive.

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:37.740
And it's in the water that the otter shows its true grace.

00:52:38.340 --> 00:52:40.900
Here in Shetland, they get a good living from the sea.

00:52:41.320 --> 00:52:42.700
taking bottom living fish.

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:46.680
such as butterfish, sea scorpion, lump sucker and rocklings.

00:52:47.620 --> 00:52:51.460
River otters also feed on fish, including a high proportion of eels.

00:52:51.720 --> 00:52:54.040
but they take a good variety of other food as well.

00:52:54.310 --> 00:52:56.990
aquatic insects, even birds and small mammals.

00:53:00.500 --> 00:53:06.020
Apart from Shetland or the west coast of Scotland, you would be extremely lucky to catch sight of an otter.

00:53:06.390 --> 00:53:09.070
In the rivers of the south, where otters used to be more common,

00:53:09.410 --> 00:53:11.490
you might instead catch sight of this animal.

00:53:12.060 --> 00:53:12.560
a mink.

00:53:13.340 --> 00:53:16.840
Animals started escaping from mink farms as early as 1929,

00:53:17.240 --> 00:53:20.300
but it's only in the last 30 years that they've bred in the wild

00:53:20.300 --> 00:53:21.600
and spread all over the country.

00:53:22.300 --> 00:53:26.600
About the same form as a stoat, they're larger and more uniformly dark.

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:29.910
and they're not so beautifully adapted to swimming as they are.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:43.390
And there's just one other animal that may cause confusion in the water.

00:53:43.830 --> 00:53:48.640
In East Anglia, there's a population of introduced coipu, rodents from South America.

00:53:49.280 --> 00:53:53.100
They're much stockier animals than otters and lack their sinuous movement.

00:53:57.220 --> 00:53:59.660
Coipus are not members of the weasel family.

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:05.400
But the badger is, and by far the largest and most rewarding of them to watch in most parts of the country.

00:54:10.170 --> 00:54:16.830
Badgers are nocturnal animals, but from May to August, in quiet spots, they emerge from their sets before dusk.

00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:20.260
Their favourite habitat is mixed woodlands and copses.

00:54:20.730 --> 00:54:25.850
But you can find their sets in hedgerows, cliffs, moorland and open fields, even in towns.

00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:29.840
They usually have well-defined paths.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:33.360
from the set and you can sometimes follow them quite long distances.

00:54:34.220 --> 00:54:36.560
You may find they lead into nearby fields.

00:54:36.940 --> 00:54:40.380
for it's there that they search for their most important single item of diet.

00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:41.620
earthworms.

00:54:44.140 --> 00:54:46.660
Badgers, however, have a very varied diet.

00:54:46.790 --> 00:54:49.510
all kinds of small mammals, fruits, beetles.

00:54:49.900 --> 00:54:53.040
and you can often find where they've been digging out a nest of insects.

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:56.700
They seem to be particularly fond of the grubs of bees and wasps.

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:00.550
